### Verdict: MAJOR

### Summary
Not approval-ready. The scorecard contract and non-baseline behavior are internally contradictory, and the launcher-preservation scope is not evidentially exact enough to verify.

### Issues Found
- `docs/plans/2026-04-22-issue-2332-provider-audit-python3-runtime-cleanup.md`, section `Prior-audit delta contract` -> `No-history / first-run behavior` says every non-baseline branch must set `python3_baseline_count = null`, but the TDD entry `test_runtime_budget_delta_fields_handle_non_baseline_states` later expects `seeded python3_baseline_count and python3_cap_count`. That makes the canonical JSON contract non-deterministic and not approval-ready.
- `docs/plans/2026-04-22-issue-2332-provider-audit-python3-runtime-cleanup.md`, sections `Existing repo code and artifacts`, `Files to Change`, and `test_first_wave_launcher_files_still_execute_with_uv_no_project_contract` are mismatched for `scripts/ai/generate-agent-radar.py`. The plan states that file already documents `uv run --no-project python ...` and is a standalone `# /// script`, yet still treats it as a first-wave bare-`python3` launcher remediation target without citing a concrete offending call site. The preservation test for that file only proves the script can be launched with `uv`; it does not verify preservation of any replaced subprocess contract.
- `docs/plans/2026-04-22-issue-2332-provider-audit-python3-runtime-cleanup.md`, section `First remediation wave` plus the launcher-preservation TDD are not exact enough for approval because only two of the three named launchers have call-site-level argv/kwargs preservation assertions. `generate-agent-radar.py` gets a smoke test instead of an exact before/after launcher-contract check, so the bounded remediation claim is not uniformly verifiable.
- `docs/plans/2026-04-22-issue-2332-provider-audit-python3-runtime-cleanup.md`, sections `Documents consulted` and `Risks and Open Questions` still contain review narration rather than plan requirements, including `the plan should use it only as prioritization context`, `this plan must close by regeneration`, `review problem will recur`, and `None blocking approval readiness`. That violates the request to keep review narration out of the plan text.

### Suggestions
- Resolve the schema contradiction in one place: decide whether `python3_baseline_count` is `null` or seeded for `no_prior_audit`, `prior_snapshot_unreadable`, and `provider_missing_from_prior_snapshot`, then make the prose, policy rules, and TDD expectations match exactly.
- For each first-wave launcher file, cite the exact in-scope executable bare-`python3` site or remove the file from remediation scope. If `scripts/ai/generate-agent-radar.py` has no such site, it should not remain in the bounded remediation list.
- Make launcher-preservation checks symmetrical across all named files. Each retained launcher target should have an exact argv/kwargs preservation assertion at the real call site, not just a smoke execution.
- Strip reviewer/meta language from the plan and keep only implementation requirements, contracts, and acceptance criteria.

### Questions for Author
- For non-baseline branches, should `python3_baseline_count` be `null`, or should it be seeded from the published phase-1 baseline/cap values?
- What exact executable bare-`python3` call inside `scripts/ai/generate-agent-radar.py` is being remediated under `#2332`?
